**Read:** [Design and Manufacturing II — MIT 2.008, 2025](../sources/Design-and-Manufacturing-II-MIT-2025.lingua#r_2FHEWYSEAHBY7X6030TW00F32E), lecture 23; follow the metallurgy powder and metal-additive Records for feedstock, thermal cycles and defect evidence.

Additive manufacture is a family of processes, not one material capability. Layer geometry, energy or deposition mechanism, supports, feedstock and postprocessing determine useful limits. Orientation can change strength, surface form, anisotropy and inspection access. A supplied mesh describes intended geometry, not delivered properties.

Compare the entire process chain: preparation, build, support removal, heat treatment, machining, cleaning and inspection. Complex internal geometry may save assembly work while making defects, retained material or repair harder to assess. Build-to-build variation needs evidence independent of one successful demonstration.

**Work:** Compare an additive and conventional version of the chosen part. Explain why the selected process can form each feature and how the critical interfaces will be finished and measured. Separate a visual prototype from a loaded service part. Use a published experiment to challenge one assumption about orientation or postprocessing.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
